# Bank 1 (runs at 0x6000). Directives: L label, C line comment, B block
# header, D data range, F row width.
#
# This bank is WELDED to its neighbours on both sides, and that is not a
# figure of speech:
#
#   - above, the code of bank 0 falls through from 0x5FFF to 0x6000. The loop
#     that starts at p00:5FFB (`push bc / call 0x6008 / pop bc`) ends right
#     here, at 0x6000-0x6007, with the `ld de,0x0008 / add ix,de / djnz /
#     ret`.
#   - below, the LAST INSTRUCTION OF THE BANK IS SPLIT. At 0x7FFE there is
#     `21 41` and the third byte, the 0x80, is the first byte of bank 2:
#     together they make `ld hl,0x8041`, and execution carries on at 0x8001.
#     It is the only place in the cartridge where this happens. Here it is
#     listed as two loose bytes because this bank's listing cannot see the
#     third one.
B 0x6000 THE BANK STARTS IN THE MIDDLE OF A LOOP
L 0x6000 end_of_bank_0_loop  The loop that starts at p00:5FFB ends here. The code crosses the bank boundary.
B 0x6008 THE ENEMY MOVERS
B 0x6008 This is the bank that moves the enemies. p00:5FFC calls 0x6008 once per
B 0x6008 object, with IX pointing at its slot, and from there it is dispatched by
B 0x6008 type (IX+0) and by the step it is on (IX+1): every enemy is a tiny state
B 0x6008 machine with its frame counter in (IX+4).
L 0x6008 run_object   Called by p00:5FFC once per object: if the slot is alive, it gives its mover one step
C 0x6008 First byte at zero: the slot is free
L 0x6014 shift_with_scroll   On steps with a new column, the object moves eight points to the left; when it goes off the edge, the slot is freed
C 0x6014 Only on the steps that bring in a column
C 0x601C Eight points to the left per step
C 0x6024 And when it goes off, the slot is freed
L 0x6029 mover_step   With the screen stopped nothing moves; types 1 and 2 shoot, and from 3 upwards it dispatches by (IX+1)
C 0x6029 0xE1C0 non-zero: the screen is stopped
C 0x602E The type is noted in 0xE123
C 0x6034 From type 3 upwards, the other dispatch
C 0x603D One frame less until the next shot
C 0x6041 Eight frames between one enemy and the next
C 0x604B Bit 0 of the type: 1 releases them from the top and 2 from the bottom
C 0x605C Type 7: the enemy that comes out of the hatch
C 0x606E The ship's X and Y, which the movers need
C 0x6079 Minus three: types 3, 4, 5 and 6
D 0x607E 0x6086 dispatcher_table_607B  Four words stuck right after the `call 0x4067` at 0x607B (0x610E, 0x6131, 0x614A, 0x615B). The index is (IX+0) minus three.
F 0x607E w2
L 0x6086 waiting_mover   Counts down and, every four frames, releases a type 0x0E shot from the enemy's position
C 0x6089 Less than 0x18 from the left edge it no longer shoots
C 0x6093 Four frames between one shot and the next
C 0x609F The table at 0x60C1: where the shot comes out for each type
C 0x60A2 The table at 0x60C1
C 0x60B2 Type 0x0E: the aimed shot
L 0x60B7 end_burst   The shots have run out: the wait until the next burst comes from the difficulty
C 0x60BA 0xE111 times two, subtracted from 0x40: the higher the difficulty, the shorter the wait
D 0x60C7 0x60CF offsets_60C1  Eight bytes that 0x609F indexes with the base 0x60C1, which falls inside the code.
F 0x60C7 8
L 0x60CF next_step   Moves the object by what the table at 0x60E7 says and leaves it 0x0D frames
C 0x60D5 Times two: two bytes per type
C 0x60E8 Thirteen frames
D 0x60ED 0x60F5 offsets_60E7  Eight bytes that 0x60D6 indexes with the base 0x60E7.
F 0x60ED 8
L 0x60F5 die_when_exhausted   When the counter reaches zero, the slot is freed
L 0x60FE set_drawing   The drawing comes from the type times two, plus one if the variant is asked for
C 0x6104 The type minus one, times two
L 0x610E check_if_passing_in_front   Waits until the ship is less than 0x40 away in X and 0x14 in Y, and then starts
C 0x6116 0x40 of margin in X
C 0x611D And 0x14 in Y
L 0x6124 start_burst   0x10 frames and 0x10 shots, and on to the next step
L 0x6131 check_if_passing_below   The same but looking 0x34 below
C 0x6131 One frame less
C 0x613C 0x40 of margin in X
C 0x6143 And 0x34 below
L 0x614A check_if_in_front   Only looks at X, with 0x18 of margin
C 0x614A One frame less
C 0x6155 0x18 of margin in X
L 0x615B check_if_already_passed   Starts as soon as the ship is left behind
C 0x615B One frame less
L 0x616A run_four_at_e800   The four objects at 0xE800, eight bytes each
C 0x616E Four objects
C 0x6177 Eight bytes per object
L 0x617F dead_call  `call 0x6014`. Nobody gets here.
D 0x617F 0x6182 dead_call  Three bytes that are `call 0x6014`. Nobody reaches 0x617F: the instruction before it ends in `ret` and no table points here.
F 0x617F 3
L 0x6182 e800_object_step   Moves with the scroll and, every four frames, uses up one unit of (IX+6); when it runs out, it switches off
C 0x6189 Four frames per unit
C 0x618D Byte 6 is used up one at a time
C 0x6190 Four frames per unit
L 0x6196 erase_e800_objects   Walks the four objects at 0xE800 erasing their drawing from the screen
L 0x6199 paint_e800_objects   The same, painting them
L 0x61A3 paint_background_objects   In stages 3 and 5 it does nothing; in the rest, it paints the objects at 0xE700
C 0x61A3 Stages 3 and 5 have their own mover
L 0x61AF paint_background_or_bank3   Stage 5 is handled by bank 3; the rest paint eight objects if it is stage 3, and two if not
C 0x61B4 Stage 5, to bank 3
C 0x61C1 Stage 3 carries eight objects; the rest, two
L 0x61CA walk_e700_objects   0xEC00 says whether to paint or erase, and then it goes through the B objects
C 0x61CA 0xEC00 at zero erases, and at 0xFF paints
C 0x61D2 Eight bytes per object
L 0x61DA erase_background_objects   The same but erasing; stage 5 is also handled by bank 3
C 0x61DA Stage 5 is handled by bank 3
C 0x61DD Stage 5 is handled by bank 3
L 0x61E8 draw_background_object   Takes the object's rectangle of characters from whichever table applies and paints or erases it
C 0x61F0 Type 0x20 uses the table at 0x633A
C 0x61F7 The rest, the one at 0x6370
C 0x61FD And in stage 2, from drawing 2 upwards it skips twelve
L 0x620D paint_rectangle   Four by four characters at the object's cell: 0x48F7 erases them and 0x490C copies them
C 0x620D Four wide by four high
C 0x6210 The cell where it lands
C 0x6219 Types 3, 4, 5 and 6 carry chunks of several sizes
C 0x621F 0xEC00 decides: erase or copy
L 0x6229 paint_in_chunks   The big types are painted in chunks: the table at 0x6283 gives the width, the height and the offset of each one
C 0x622D Times three: three bytes per chunk
C 0x6236 The width, the height and where it lands
C 0x623C Bit 7 of the third byte shifts the chunk by half a cell
C 0x6246 Bit 7 of the third byte shifts the chunk by half a cell
L 0x6256 paint_wide_chunk   A ten by one rectangle, with the origin taken from the two tables at 0x6273 and 0x64D4
C 0x6259 Times two: two bytes per drawing
C 0x625A The table at 0x6273
C 0x626D Times ten: ten characters per row
C 0x6275 Ten wide by one high
D 0x627B 0x6283 table_6273  Eight bytes that 0x625A indexes with the base 0x6273.
F 0x627B 8
D 0x6283 0x62AD table_6283  Forty-two bytes read by 0x6230.
F 0x6283 8
L 0x62AD release_background_objects   Keeps taking out background objects while the script has rows for this distance
C 0x62B0 Stage 5 is handled by bank 3
C 0x62BE 0xE109 moves on to the next row
L 0x62C1 release_background_objects_on_column   The same, but only on the steps that bring in a new column
C 0x62C1 Only on the steps with a column
C 0x62CE 0xF8 in 0xEC04
L 0x62D9 check_background_script   Checks the script at 0x64FA with the bank 2 routine and, when due, sets up the object in the first free slot at 0xE700
C 0x62DF The bank 2 routine that compares the distance
C 0x62E7 The eight slots at 0xE700
C 0x62EC Eight bytes per slot
C 0x62F0 The first free slot
C 0x62F1 Eight bytes per slot
C 0x62F3 Eight bytes: the next slot
