B 0x6677 THE ENEMY'S AIM GETS WORSE ON PURPOSE... WITH THE R REGISTER
B 0x6677 From difficulty 7 upwards, the enemy's shot does NOT go straight at the
B 0x6677 ship: it gets an error taken from the Z80's R register, four bits, which
B 0x6677 is sometimes added and sometimes subtracted depending on bit 6 of that
B 0x6677 same read. Below difficulty 7 the shot is clean. It is the fourth time
B 0x6677 the cartridge uses the refresh register as a number generator.
L 0x6677 aim_at_ship   Works out which way the shot goes; from difficulty 7 upwards it adds an error from the R register
C 0x667E Below difficulty 7, no error
C 0x6685 The R register: four bits of error
C 0x668A And its bit 6 decides whether it is added or subtracted
C 0x668C Bit 6 decides the sign of the error
C 0x6698 Capped at 0x3F
B 0x669F THE SINE AND THE COSINE COME FROM THE SAME TABLE
B 0x669F To know which way the shot goes it needs the sine and the cosine of the
B 0x669F angle, and the cartridge does not keep two tables: it keeps ONE, the one
B 0x669F at 0x6853, and reads it twice. Once with the index as it is and once
B 0x669F with 0x3F minus the index, which is exactly the complementary angle.
B 0x669F With that it gets the two components, multiplies them by the speed
B 0x669F (0x6731, with the hand-made eight by eight multiplication at 0x6743)
B 0x669F and gives them the right sign with the two's complement at 0x6729.
L 0x669F get_both_speeds   Reads the table at 0x6853 from both sides (the index and its complement to 0x3F) and from that come the two speeds, the vertical and the horizontal
C 0x66A2 0x3F minus the index: the complementary angle
C 0x66A6 The table at 0x6853, read from both ends
C 0x66A9 The table, from both ends
C 0x66B4 Times the speed
C 0x66B7 0xEC10 says whether X goes the other way
C 0x66BE 0xEC12: the vertical speed, the one from the difference in rows
C 0x66D0 And 0xEC14: the horizontal one
L 0x66D5 measure_distance_to_ship   Gets the two differences as absolute values, notes their signs in 0xEC10 and 0xEC11, and from the pair gets the angle
C 0x66DA 0xE204: the ship's row
C 0x66E0 When negative, the sign is noted
C 0x66E9 0xE206: its column
C 0x66F8 With the sum of the two below 0x30, the ship is right on top
C 0x6701 The high nibble of one and of the other: the index into the table at 0x6753
C 0x670F 0xEC17 keeps the angle
C 0x671E And 0xEC18 the quadrant, which comes from the two signs
L 0x6729 change_sign   Two's complement of DE
C 0x6729 Two's complement: the other direction
L 0x6731 times_speed   Multiplies the component by the speed in 0xE110 and keeps the high part, shifted three bits
C 0x6731 0xE110: the shot's speed
C 0x6739 Doubled three times, keeping the carry
C 0x673A And times two once more
L 0x6743 multiply_h_by_e   Hand-made 8 by 8 multiplication: eight `add hl,hl` adding DE when there is a carry. Returns the product in HL.
C 0x6743 Eight rounds of shift and add: the classic
D 0x674F 0x6753 table_674F  Four bytes ahead of the base 0x6753.
F 0x674F 4
D 0x6753 0x6853 angles  Two hundred and fifty-six bytes that 0x6708 indexes with the high nibble of one difference and that of the other: from the pair (dx, dy) comes the angle with which the quarter sine at 0x6853 is then read.
F 0x6753 16
D 0x6853 0x6893 quarter_sine  Sixty-four bytes that are A QUARTER OF A SINE, from 0 to 255: compared with 255*sin(k/63*90) the maximum error is 1.9. 0x66A6 reads them TWICE, once with the index and once with 0x3F minus the index, and that way gets the sine and the cosine of the same angle from a single table; the Pythagorean check (t[k]^2 plus t[63-k]^2) gives between 0.98 and 1.00 times 255 squared.
F 0x6853 16
