B 0x6A72 PUTTING A NEW OBJECT ON THE SCREEN
B 0x6A72 This is the most called routine in the bank: all the enemy movers use it
B 0x6A72 to release a shot, an explosion or a new enemy. It is entered with A =
B 0x6A72 the type, DE = where it goes and C = an adjustment, and it looks for a
B 0x6A72 free slot in the object table, fills it with the record that belongs to
B 0x6A72 the type (four bytes from the table at 0x6BA3) and bumps the live count.
B 0x6A72 Three types break the rule: 0x1E needs THREE consecutive slots (which
B 0x6A72 is why 0x6AA1 looks for three consecutive free slots and adds two to the
B 0x6A72 count), 0x0E goes to another table (the one at 0xE460, eight slots of
B 0x6A72 0x20 bytes counted backwards) and the rest to the one at 0xE300, twelve
B 0x6A72 slots.
L 0x6A72 spawn_object   Looks for a free slot and sets up there an object of type A, at position DE
C 0x6A72 Where it goes, noted in 0xEC19
C 0x6A76 And the type, in 0xE120
C 0x6A84 Type 0x1E takes three slots
C 0x6A89 And 0x0E goes to the other table
C 0x6A8D The twelve slots at 0xE300, 0x20 apart
L 0x6A98 find_three_consecutive_slots   The big object needs three consecutive free slots; if there are none, it does not come out
