B 0x6CEF THE END OF EACH STAGE, ONE PER STAGE
B 0x6CEF The table at 0x6CD7 dispatches by stage: each one has its own little
B 0x6CEF state machine here, with the step in 0xE065, and they all follow the
B 0x6CEF same script: wait for the distance travelled to reach a point, switch on
B 0x6CEF the boss (0xE151), wait for it to be killed (0xE150) and let the game
B 0x6CEF move on to the next stage. What changes from one to another is the
B 0x6CEF distances and which bank 2 or 3 routine is called.
L 0x6CEF end_of_stage_1   Waits for distance 0x165, switches on the warning in 0xE140 and leaves the limit at 0x1C2
C 0x6CEF 0xE065: the end-of-stage step
C 0x6CF2 0xE065: the end-of-stage step
C 0x6D01 The distance travelled...
C 0x6D04 ...against 0x165
C 0x6D0B Only on the steps with a new column
C 0x6D15 And the scroll stops at 0x1C2
L 0x6D1B advance_end_step   0xE065 + 1
L 0x6D20 switch_off_boss   With 0xE150 set, switches off the flag and the warning in 0xE1B0
C 0x6D20 0xE150: not until it dies
L 0x6D2C wait_until_dead   Until 0xE150 is set, it does not go on; then it lets the scroll run up to 0x1C0
C 0x6D2C 0xE150: not until it dies
C 0x6D2F 0xE150: not until it dies
C 0x6D36 The scroll goes up to 0x1C0
L 0x6D3E switch_on_boss   0xE151 to one: the boss comes on screen
C 0x6D43 0xE150 to zero and 0xE114 to one
C 0x6D48 0xE114 to one
C 0x6D4B And 0xE151 and 0xE152: the boss on its first step
B 0x6D53 THE ORDER OF THE STAGES IS NOT 1, 2, 3...
B 0x6D53 There are two different ways out of a stage, and that is where the
B 0x6D53 cartridge's real route comes from:
B 0x6D53   - end_stage (0x6D53) calls go_to_next_stage, which adds
B 0x6D53     one to 0xE061 and wraps around after the eighth.
B 0x6D53   - jump_to_stage (0x6FB9) puts a number written by hand into 0xE061,
B 0x6D53     and EIGHT places jump to it, each with its own number.
B 0x6D53 Stages 2, 3, 4 and 7 look at 0xE1C0 (the screen stopped) and, if it is,
B 0x6D53 jump to 9, 10, 11 and 12; and those four, when they end, jump to 3, 4,
B 0x6D53 5 and 8, which are the four bytes of stages_per_round.
B 0x6D53 So the twelve stages are played like this:
B 0x6D53     1 - 2 - 9 - 3 - 10 - 4 - 11 - 5 - 6 - 7 - 12 - 8 - ending - 1
B 0x6D53 The four two-digit ones are interludes slipped in between the others,
B 0x6D53 not a second loop nor a loose stretch.
